{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I reapply after a visa refusal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, in almost all cases you can reapply. The key is understanding exactly why the refusal happened and fixing that specific issue — reapplying with the same weak documents rarely helps."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to speak fluently in the interview?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No — officers are assessing your intent and documentation, not your accent or fluency. Clear, honest, and consistent answers matter far more than perfect English."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I bring printed or digital documents?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Printed, organised copies are safest. Some embassies accept digital documents, but a physical folder in a clear order avoids any risk of a phone dying or a file not opening on the day."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What if my bank balance fluctuated recently?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Be ready to explain any large or unusual transactions with a simple paper trail. Unexplained large deposits just before applying are one of the most common red flags."}}
